a new caravan of some 1800 Central  Americans is making its way towards the  u.s. this morning they started crossing  in New Mexico from Guatemala late last  week and continued arriving through the  weekend Adriana Diaz is in Co dad  Hidalgo Mexico on the Mexican side of  the bridge where migrants enter from  Guatemala  Adriana good morning good morning the  migrants are camped out here as far as  you can see on this bridge that side of  the bridge is Guatemala we are on the  Mexican side there waiting to be  officially registered by the Mexican  government in a new process that just  started a week ago in preparation for  this Caravan what happens is is when  people arrive they receive wristbands to  register them their identity is verified  and then five days later they can  receive humanitarian visas allowing them  to stay in Mexico for a year to work and  to live now this is a stark contrast to  the last major caravan that passed  through in October that Caravan at its  height had more than 7,000 people and  clashed with police on this very same  bridge the bridge was so packed that  people actually chose to jump off the  bridge to try to swim to Mexico as for  where that caravan stands roughly 2600  people made it to the border tried to  cross illegally and were arrested by  u.s. Border Patrol about 1300 didn't get  that far they chose to go back to their  home countries essentially giving up  nearly 3,000 people received Mexican  visas and fewer than 700 people remain  at Tijuana trying to seek asylum we've  spoken to people here this morning who  told us they found out about this latest  caravan on Facebook they're trying to  head north to the US border one father  actually told us that he saw I was  American and he said will they let us in  with our children Nora  alright Adriana thank you
 

